#
# prima-materia — the searches of the run on the Heptameron, 2026-10-13
#
# In a file of their own for the mechanical reason recorded at
# examinations/searches-jain.ttl and in prima-materia#703.
#

pmp:HeptameronFailureSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the Heptameron names a sign, a term or a count by which the operator would know the operation had failed"@en, "Ob das Heptameron ein Zeichen, eine Frist oder eine Zahl nennt, an der der Operierende das Misslingen der Operation erkennen würde"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Elementa magica Petri de Abano, complete, in the Marburg print bound after the Liber quartus de occulta philosophia, read at the wording in Latin; the same text in Robert Turner's English, London 1655, in the facsimile of 1978; the passages entered checked in the Opera omnia of 1600, volume I."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any clause stating what the operator would observe if the spirits did not come or the thing asked did not follow, a term after which nothing is to be expected, a number of attempts, a fault in the operator or the procedure named as the cause of a failure, or an instruction to stop."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the Latin, in place and in the text layer: si non, nisi, quod si, frustra, nihil, nil, non poterit, deficere, fallacia, error, iterum, dies, hora, every conditional particle of the chapter on the manner of working and of the visions; in Turner's English: if, unless, not, fail, again, in vain, error, and every conditional of the same chapters. The seven day sections read complete in both."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-13" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-13" ;
    skos:note "Nothing, and the nothing is exact. The only conditional clauses of the text are three: the vestment and the Moon, each with if it can be done, entered at pmc:HeptameronSiFieriPotest; and if the spirits are obstinate, use the exorcisms that follow, entered at pmc:HeptameronPertinaces. The only consequence the text names for a case in which nothing appears falls on the spirits, at pmc:HeptameronComminatio. The word fallacia occurs once, in the binding that the spirits are to perform without deceit, and it excludes the case instead of foreseeing it."@en .

pmp:HeptameronBodilyWaiverSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the Heptameron releases anyone from the nine days, the confession, the communion or the three days of fasting"@en, "Ob das Heptameron jemanden von den neun Tagen, der Beichte, der Kommunion oder den drei Fastentagen befreit"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The same text in the same three prints."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A release from the requirements on the operator's body and conduct, of the kind the text grants for the waxing Moon and the priest's vestment, for any operator or under any circumstance, and any exemption for the experienced of the kind the Clavicula grants at pmc:ClaviculaAdeptiExempti."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "si fieri potest, si non possit, nisi, vel, aut, peritus, provectus, doctus, rudis, in the Latin; if it may, if it cannot, or, learned, experienced, ignorant, in Turner's English; the preface, which divides the readers into the learned and the ignorant, read with them."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-13" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-13" ;
    skos:note "No release from the bodily and conduct requirements. The one place where the text sorts its readers by experience, the preface, does so to explain why the book exists, for the ignorant who need the ceremonies in detail; it does not exempt the learned from them, unlike the Clavicula."@en .
